  "post": {
    "caption": "A squid's cousin became a gemstone. Ammolite comes from the fossil shells of ammonites, extinct sea animals that were distant cousins of squid and octopus. They lived about 70 to 75 million years ago, and gem-quality ammolite is mined commercially only in southern Alberta. Stacked layers of aragonite bend light into rainbow colors, and red and green are the most common while blue is rare. Jewelers usually back the thin layer with shale and cap it with clear quartz. The Blackfoot call fossil ammonite stones iniskim, or buffalo stones, and in one story a singing stone called the buffalo back. In 2022, Alberta made ammolite its official gemstone.",
